[# Section 2 — the CX data language.
   Authored 2026-05-22. Anchors normative depth in spec/cxdm.md,
   spec/canonical.md, spec/grammar.ebnf, spec/core/data-bin.md, spec/schema.md,
   spec/namespaces.md, spec/include.md, spec/i18n.md, spec/streaming.md.
   File extension: .cxd (data-only source, per d-2026-05-22-05). #]

[section id=data n=2 title='The data language'

  [intro
    """CX's data language is what you write in a `.cx` or `.cxd` file when
       you mean "this is data." It is also what every CX code expression
       evaluates to — programs and values share one shape. This section is
       the working reference for the data surface; the normative grammar
       is in [`spec/grammar.ebnf`](../../spec/grammar.ebnf) and the value
       model is in [`spec/cxdm.md`](../../spec/cxdm.md)."""]

  [child id=lexical n=2.1 title='Lexical structure'
    [intro
      """Every CX source file is UTF-8 text. The lexer is small: identifiers,
         scalar literals, brackets (`[`, `]`), parentheses (used for sequences
         and argument lists), curly braces (used for map literals), sigils,
         strings, and comments. The full lexical rules are in
         [`spec/grammar.ebnf`](../../spec/grammar.ebnf)."""]

    [child id=identifiers n=2.1.1 title='Identifiers'
      [body
        """An identifier starts with an ASCII letter or underscore and
           continues with letters, digits, underscores, and hyphens. Hyphens
           are valid identifier characters (CX leans on the kebab-case
           convention used by Lisp, Clojure, and Scheme). The reserved tokens
           `true`, `false`, `null`, and the directive names (starting with
           `?`) are excluded. See [[reserved-tokens]] for the complete
           reserved-name list."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[user-profile first-name='Ada' last-name='Lovelace']"""]
      [body
        """Names of CX elements are stored as Unicode code-point sequences;
           a single canonical byte form is used at the wire level (see
           [[canonical]]). Unicode normalization is NFC — composed form is
           the canonical shape."""]]

    [child id=comments n=2.1.2 title='Comments (line and block)'
      [body
        """CX has one comment form: `[# … #]`. It nests, it spans any number
           of lines, and it is allowed anywhere whitespace is allowed. The
           comment is a real lexical element, not a hack on top of a string
           — the parser drops it before AST construction. Comments are
           preserved by the lossless canonicalizer (`cx fmt`) and stripped
           by the strict canonicalizer (`cx canon`); see [[canonical]]."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[# A single-line note. #]
           [config
             [# This sub-comment is fine, even
                [# nested #] inside another. #]
             [host 'localhost']]"""]
      [note
        """Older CX dialects used `[-` `-]` for block comments. v0.8.0 keeps
           the `[# #]` form only. See [[migration]] for the rename."""]]

    [child id=whitespace n=2.1.3 title='Whitespace'
      [body
        """Whitespace separates tokens; otherwise it is not significant.
           Indentation does not change meaning. CX is bracket-structured, so
           you can compact a document to one line, or pretty-print it across
           many — the canonical form (see [[cx-canonical]]) is unique and
           hash-stable either way."""]
      [body
        """Inside element bodies, runs of whitespace are collapsed to single
           spaces by default. Inside triple-quoted strings whitespace is
           preserved verbatim. The `xml:space=preserve` attribute (see
           [[xml-space]]) overrides the default within its scope."""]]]

  [child id=elements n=2.2 title='Elements'
    [intro
      """An **element** is the basic CX data structure: a name, zero or more
         attributes, and a body. Every bracketed form is an element. The
         normative production is [`spec/grammar.ebnf`](../../spec/grammar.ebnf)
         `[51] Element`."""]

    [child id=names n=2.2.1 title='Element names'
      [body
        """The first token inside `[` is the element name. It is an
           identifier (or a string literal, if you need spaces, but that is
           unusual in idiomatic CX). Names starting with `?` denote
           directives (CX code, not data) — see [[code]]."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[server]                       [# empty element                 #]
           [server host='a.com']          [# attributes only               #]
           [server [host 'a.com']]        [# nested elements as body       #]"""]]

    [child id=attributes n=2.2.2 title='Attributes'
      [body
        """Attributes are key-value pairs in the form `name=value`. The key
           is an identifier; the value is a bare scalar, a quoted string,
           a numeric literal, a boolean literal, a date, or `null`. Multiple
           attributes are space-separated within the element header."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[user id=42 email='ada@example.com' active=true]"""]
      [note
        """The `:name value` form is NOT element-attribute syntax. It is
           directive labeled-slot syntax (per `spec/grammar.ebnf [127e]`)
           used inside `[?directive ...]`. On a plain data element you write
           `name=value`. The two forms are not interchangeable."""]
      [body
        """Attribute order is preserved by the parser but is **not** part
           of the canonical identity — re-ordering attributes does not
           change the SHA-256 of the canonical bytes. See [[canonical]]
           for the normalization rules. Three sigil-prefixed attribute
           shortcuts exist: `+name` (= `name=true`), `-name` (= `name=false`),
           `#name` (the id sigil, see [[id]]). The anchor sigil `&name` and
           the merge sigil `*name` are also attribute-level — see [[anchor]]
           and [[merge]]."""]]

    [child id=bodies n=2.2.3 title='Body content'
      [body
        """The body of an element is everything between the attribute list
           and the closing `]`. A body may be empty, a single scalar, a
           sequence of scalars and elements (mixed content / prose — see
           [[prose]]), or a sequence of just elements."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[empty]
           [scalar 42]
           [prose 'Some ' [em 'mixed'] ' content.']
           [list [item 1] [item 2] [item 3]]"""]
      [body
        """A bare scalar body is auto-typed per the rules in
           [`spec/ast.md`](../../spec/ast.md) §Scalar — `42` becomes `:int`,
           `3.14` becomes `:float`, `2026-05-22` becomes `:date`, etc. To
           force a type, prefix it: `[port :u16 8080]`."""]]]

  [child id=scalars n=2.3 title='Scalar kinds'
    [intro
      """CX has **eight** scalar kinds per [`spec/cxdm.md`](../../spec/cxdm.md)
         §2.2: string, int, float, bool, date, datetime, bytes, null. The
         wire encoding for each kind is in
         [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md) §3. Each kind can be
         introduced literally (e.g. `42` for int) or with an explicit type
         tag (e.g. `:int 42` or `:u32 42`)."""]

    [child id=strings n=2.3.1 title='Strings — single, double, triple-quoted'
      [body
        """Strings come in three forms. Single-quoted (`'…'`) and
           double-quoted (`"…"`) work the same way and support escapes.
           Triple-quoted (`'''…'''` or `\"\"\"…\"\"\"`) strings span lines,
           preserve internal whitespace, and need no escaping for the other
           quote characters."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[greeting 'Hello, world']
           [path "C:\\Users\\Ada"]
           [readme \"\"\"
             This is a
             multi-line string
             with 'embedded' quotes.
           \"\"\"]"""]
      [body
        """Triple-quoted strings are the default for any body text longer
           than one line. They are the building block for the prose form
           (see [[prose]]) and for the `[?modify]` action `:set` values
           when those values are documents in their own right."""]
      [body
        """The canonical form (`cx canon`) emits single-quoted strings for
           single-line content and triple-double-quoted strings for
           multi-line content. Escapes are minimised to what the parser
           strictly requires."""]]

    [child id=numbers n=2.3.2 title='Numbers — int, float, sized, hex'
      [body
        """Integer literals are plain digits, optionally signed:
           `42`, `-17`, `0`. Float literals contain a decimal point or
           exponent: `3.14`, `1e9`, `-2.5e-3`. Hex literals are written
           `0x…`. The default `:int` is a 64-bit signed integer; `:float`
           is IEEE-754 double. Sized variants are available as type-tagged
           scalars when binding to host languages where the size matters;
           see [[sized-types]]."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[answer 42]
           [pi 3.14159]
           [big 1e18]
           [color 0xff8800]
           [port :u16 8080]
           [pixel-density :f32 1.5]"""]
      [body
        """Special float values: `nan`, `inf`, `-inf` are recognised. NaN
           does not equal NaN under `=` (per IEEE-754); the `is-nan`
           builtin tests for it explicitly. `-0.0` and `+0.0` compare
           equal under `=` but produce different canonical bytes."""]
      [body
        """Integers do not auto-promote to a larger type on overflow.
           Adding `1` to `9223372036854775807` (max i64) raises CXER0103
           (numeric overflow). For arbitrary-precision arithmetic use the
           `:bigint` type tag with a string-form literal; the v0.8.0 builtin
           library does not include BigInt arithmetic — that is a
           post-v0.8.0 follow-up."""]]

    [child id=booleans n=2.3.3 title='Booleans'
      [body
        """`true` and `false`. They are reserved tokens; the identifier
           `true` always parses as the boolean true value. Use sigil
           forms (see [[bool]]) for compact attribute syntax."""]]

    [child id=dates n=2.3.4 title='Dates and datetimes'
      [body
        """Dates are ISO-8601 calendar dates: `2026-05-22`. Datetimes are
           ISO-8601 with time and optional zone offset:
           `2026-05-22T14:30:00Z` or `2026-05-22T14:30:00-04:00`. Both are
           first-class scalars — they round-trip across every projection
           and have stable canonical byte encodings. See
           [[datetime-detail]] for timezone, precision, and leap-second
           semantics."""]]

    [child id=null n=2.3.5 title='Null'
      [body
        """`null` is the absent-value scalar. Distinct from the empty
           sequence `()`. `null` is a value; `()` is the absence of any
           value. CXPath predicates and `[?if]` treat them differently —
           `null` is a single-item sequence containing the null scalar;
           `()` is an empty sequence. The effective-boolean-value rule
           (cxdm §4.6) treats `null` as truthy iff its containing element
           exists; treats `()` as always falsy."""]]

    [child id=bytes n=2.3.6 title='Bytes — raw binary scalars'
      [body
        """`:bytes` is the eighth scalar kind: a length-prefixed byte
           sequence with no character semantics. Use it for image data,
           hashes, encrypted payloads, or any opaque binary value."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[hash :bytes 0x3a7bd3e2360a3d29eea436fcfb7e44c735d117c42d1c1835420b6b9942dd4f1b]
           [logo :bytes base64('iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAAB...')]"""]
      [body
        """Bytes literals accept three syntaxes: a hex string prefixed `0x`,
           or the `base64('…')` / `hex('…')` builtin constructors. The
           canonical form emits the hex string with the `0x` prefix; the
           data-bin wire format uses raw length-prefixed bytes (no
           encoding overhead). Each projection has its own bytes form:
           JSON emits a `{"_cx_bytes": "base64..."}` envelope (no native
           bytes in JSON); YAML uses `!!binary base64...`; XML uses CDATA
           with base64 content; Markdown uses a data URI in a code fence.
           See [[surfaces]] §3.10 (bytes wire forms) for the full table."""]]

    [child id=sized-types n=2.3.7 title='Sized type tags'
      [body
        """When binding CX to host languages where integer size matters
           (Rust `i32` vs `i64`, Go `uint8` vs `int`, C `int16_t`),
           a sized type tag is attached to the scalar. The full set:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Signed integers:** `:i8`, `:i16`, `:i32`, `:i64`."""]
        [item """**Unsigned integers:** `:u8`, `:u16`, `:u32`, `:u64`."""]
        [item """**Floats:** `:f32`, `:f64`."""]
        [item """**Default-int:** `:int` — alias for `:i64`."""]
        [item """**Default-float:** `:float` — alias for `:f64`."""]]
      [body
        """A literal without a type tag is auto-typed as `:int` (for whole
           numbers) or `:float` (for numbers with `.` or `e`). To force a
           sized variant: `[port :u16 8080]`, `[pixel-density :f32 1.5]`.
           Sized variants flow through ast-bin and data-bin with their
           type identifier preserved; bindings translate to the host's
           native numeric type."""]
      [body
        """Overflow on a sized scalar (writing `300` into `:u8`, or
           `2147483648` into `:i32`) raises CXER0103 at validation time.
           Bindings raise the host's equivalent error (e.g. Python
           `OverflowError`, Rust `TryFromIntError`)."""]]]

  [child id=collections n=2.4 title='Collection literals'
    [intro
      """CX has three collection literal forms: **sequence**, **array**,
         and **map**. The distinction is load-bearing for function
         signatures — see [`spec/cxdm.md`](../../spec/cxdm.md) §2 D18 and
         [[container-atom]] below."""]

    [child id=seq n=2.4.1 title='Sequence literals (a, b, c)'
      [body
        """A comma-separated parenthesized list is a sequence: `(1, 2, 3)`.
           Sequences are flat — concatenating sequences never produces a
           sequence-of-sequences (the XQuery sequence-flat rule, see
           [[seq-flat]])."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[ports (80, 443, 8080)]"""]
      [body
        """A sequence with one element is equivalent to that single
           element: `(42)` ≡ `42`. The empty sequence is `()` — distinct
           from `null` (see [[null]])."""]]

    [child id=arr n=2.4.2 title='Array literals [a, b, c]'
      [body
        """A bracketed comma-separated list (distinguished from an element
           by the absence of a name and the presence of commas) is an
           array. Arrays preserve nesting — an array of arrays stays
           shaped that way."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[matrix [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]]"""]
      [body
        """Arrays index from zero in CXPath and the host-language
           bindings (`$arr[0]`). Negative indexing is supported in Python
           and Rust Layer-2 idioms; the Layer-1 surface uses
           `arr.at(n)` (0-based, errors on out-of-bounds)."""]]

    [child id=map n=2.4.3 title='Map literals {k: v}'
      [body
        """Curly braces wrap a map literal: keys are atomic scalars, values
           are any item. Keys must be unique; duplicate keys at parse time
           raise CXER0102. Insertion order is preserved for round-trip
           but not for canonical identity — two maps with the same
           key-value pairs hash to the same canonical bytes regardless of
           insertion order."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[env {host: 'a.com', port: 8080, tls: true}]"""]
      [body
        """The map key type is `ScalarKey` per cxdm §2: bool, int, float,
           string, date, datetime, or bytes. `null` is not a valid key
           (`null = null` would not satisfy the uniqueness invariant
           under value-comparison semantics). Mixed-type keys are
           permitted; comparison is by canonical bytes of each key."""]]

    [child id=seq-flat n=2.4.4 title='The sequence-flat principle'
      [body
        """**Every value in CX is a sequence; sequences do not nest at the
           sequence boundary.** This is XQuery's sequence-flat rule
           (cxdm §1) — the property that makes the expression family
           compose."""]
      [list
        [item """`//user` returns a sequence whether the document has 0,
                1, or N users — consumers iterate uniformly."""]
        [item """Concatenation is total and associative:
                `concat(a, b, c)` never raises "can't concatenate
                scalar to list"."""]
        [item """A directive's return value is "what it evaluates to";
                multiple emits naturally concatenate."""]]
      [body
        """What sequence-flat is NOT: a list-of-lists model. Concatenating
           two sequences yields one flat sequence, never a
           sequence-containing-two-sub-sequences. To preserve nesting use
           **arrays** ([[arr]]) — arrays are container Items, not
           sequence boundaries."""]]

    [child id=container-atom n=2.4.5 title='Container vs atom distinction'
      [body
        """cxdm v1.1 introduces a load-bearing distinction between **atom**
           Items (Node, Scalar) and **container** Items (Array, Map,
           Sequence-as-Item) per cxdm §2.0:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Atoms** are leaf values from the value-model perspective.
                A Scalar `42` is a single Item; a Node is a single Item even
                though its tree contents are richly structured."""]
        [item """**Containers** hold further Items by composition at the
                value level. `[1, 2, 3]` is one Array Item that holds three
                Scalar Items. `{name: 'a'}` is one Map Item that holds one
                (key, value) pair."""]]
      [body
        """This governs which operations apply where. Sequence operations
           (`count`, `head`, `tail`, `concat`, `distinct`, `union`,
           `intersect`, `except`) take **Sequence** and do not implicitly
           descend into a container — `count(arr)` raises a type error;
           the explicit form is `count(items(arr))` or
           `count(flatten(arr))`. Array operations (`array:size`,
           indexing) take **Array** and treat it as a single value. Map
           operations (`map:keys`, `map:get`) take **Map**."""]
      [body
        """The practical impact: function signatures type-check at the
           value-model layer. A function that expects a sequence will not
           silently accept an array (or vice versa); the caller writes the
           conversion explicitly, making intent visible to readers and
           tools."""]]]

  [child id=sigils n=2.5 title='Sigils'
    [intro
      """Sigils are one-character prefixes that change how a token is
         interpreted. CX has five element-level sigils: `&` (anchor),
         `*` (merge), `#` (id), `+`/`-` (compact booleans). The `:` is
         overloaded for type tags (scalar position) and for directive
         labeled slots (inside `[?...]`)."""]

    [child id=anchor n=2.5.1 title='The & anchor sigil'
      [body
        """`&name` declares a reusable shape anchor on an element. Other
           elements can refer to it later in the same document via the
           merge sigil `*name`. Anchors are document-scoped — they
           resolve within the same document and do not cross include
           boundaries (per [[include-capabilities]])."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[defaults &server-base host='localhost' tls=true]
           [service-a *server-base port=8001]
           [service-b *server-base port=8002]"""]
      [body
        """An anchor declaration with `&name` MAY appear at most once per
           document; duplicate `&name` declarations raise CXER0101. The
           lossless canonicalizer preserves anchors and aliases; the strict
           canonicalizer expands them and the canonical bytes are the
           expanded form."""]]

    [child id=merge n=2.5.2 title='The * merge sigil'
      [body
        """`*name` expands the anchor's attributes and body into the
           current element. Local attributes override merged ones, so you
           can specialize. Merge resolution is depth-first and stops at
           cycles; cyclic `*` references raise CXER0101."""]]

    [child id=id n=2.5.3 title='The # id sigil'
      [body
        """`#name` declares an identity on the element. Identities are
           document-unique and can be referenced via CXPath `id('name')`.
           Identities differ from anchors: anchors are for shape reuse
           (data-level), identities are for cross-references (semantic
           link). Full semantics in
           [`spec/identity.md`](../../spec/identity.md)."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[user #ada email='ada@example.com']
           [post author-id=#ada \"\"\"First post\"\"\"]"""]
      [body
        """A `#name` reference (as in `author-id=#ada` above) resolves to
           the element bearing `#ada`. The reference does not embed; it
           remains a pointer. CXPath `id('ada')` selects the target
           element. Cross-format identity rules: IDs survive round-trip
           through every projection — JSON/YAML/TOML/XML/MD all have or
           can construct an `id` key. See [[identity]]."""]]

    [child id=type n=2.5.4 title='The : type sigil'
      [body
        """`:` is reserved for two surface roles. In **value position**
           (`[port :u16 8080]`) it introduces a type tag (the long-name
           ascription form `::T` is the canonical spelling — `:u16` here
           is the short-name shorthand). In **atom position** (`:ok`,
           `:not-found`) it introduces a symbolic atom literal. The
           parser disambiguates by context. The `:` sigil is no
           longer used to introduce labeled directive slots — every
           clause is now an explicit child element (`[then …]`,
           `[else …]`, `[yield …]`, `[where …]`)."""]
      [body
        """Type tags are explicit; the eight scalar kinds (see [[scalars]])
           plus sized variants (see [[sized-types]]) all use the `:` sigil
           when written explicitly. The CXPath `instance of` operator uses
           the same vocabulary: `$x instance of :int`."""]]

    [child id=bool n=2.5.5 title='The + and - bool sigils'
      [body
        """`+name` is shorthand for `name=true`; `-name` for
           `name=false`. They keep terse attribute lists readable."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[user email='ada@example.com' +active -banned]
           [# equivalent to:
              [user email='ada@example.com' active=true banned=false] #]"""]]]

  [child id=prose n=2.6 title='Mixed content and prose'
    [intro
      """Mixed content — text interleaved with elements — is first-class
         CX, not a separate "string mode". The body of any element may
         alternate freely between scalars and child elements. This makes
         Markdown projection lossless: a paragraph with inline code is
         stored as a `[p]` element containing a text run, a `[code]`
         element, and more text."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[para
         \"\"\"The function \"\"\"
         [code 'parse']
         \"\"\" returns a \"\"\"
         [link to='spec/cxdm.md' 'Document']
         \"\"\" value.\"\"\"]"""]

    [child id=entity-refs n=2.6.1 title='Entity references'
      [body
        """CX text accepts the five XML/HTML entity references for
           characters that would otherwise terminate a token: `&amp;`,
           `&lt;`, `&gt;`, `&quot;`, `&apos;`. They are decoded at parse
           time; the canonical form re-encodes only what the parser
           strictly requires (e.g. `&` becomes `&amp;` only when it sits
           inside an unquoted attribute value or near a `[` that would
           start an element)."""]
      [body
        """Numeric character references (`&#65;` for `A`, `&#x41;` for the
           same) are accepted on input and re-encoded as the literal
           character on canonical emit. External entity references (the
           `<!ENTITY foo SYSTEM "..."> &foo;` pattern from XML) are
           accepted on parse but resolution is gated by the include
           capability bit — see [[include-capabilities]]."""]]

    [child id=xml-space n=2.6.2 title='Whitespace preservation (xml:space)'
      [body
        """By default CX collapses runs of whitespace in element bodies
           to single spaces. The `xml:space=preserve` attribute on an
           element overrides this within its scope: whitespace inside the
           element (and its descendants until a redeclaration) is
           preserved verbatim."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[poem xml:space=preserve
           \"\"\"  Roses are red,
             Violets are blue,
              Whitespace preserved,
               just for you.\"\"\"]"""]
      [body
        """`xml:space=default` re-enables the default collapse behavior in
           a nested scope. The attribute survives XML round-trip
           identically; in JSON/YAML/TOML it round-trips as a regular
           attribute."""]]

    [child id=cdata n=2.6.3 title='CDATA sections'
      [body
        """XML CDATA blocks (`<![CDATA[...]]>`) project to triple-quoted
           strings on CX import; the round-trip back to XML re-emits a
           CDATA block when the content contains characters that would
           otherwise require escaping. CX itself has no separate CDATA
           syntax — triple-quoted is the universal verbatim form for any
           body content."""]]]

  [child id=tables n=2.7 title='Typed columnar tables'
    [intro
      """For analytical workloads CX has a typed columnar table form. The
         logical shape is `[name :table :columns (col-spec, ...) row-tuples...]`;
         the physical wire format is CXCol (see [[cxcol]]) and the
         normative table API is in
         [`spec/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/misc/table-api.md)."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[orders :table
         :columns (id :int, customer :string, amount :float, ts :datetime)
         (1, 'Ada',   42.50, 2026-05-22T10:00:00Z)
         (2, 'Grace', 17.25, 2026-05-22T10:15:00Z)
         (3, 'Linus', 99.00, 2026-05-22T11:30:00Z)]"""]
    [body
      """**Column spec:** each column has a name and a type tag. Type tags
         use the full scalar vocabulary (`:int`, `:string`, `:float`,
         `:bool`, `:date`, `:datetime`, `:bytes`, plus sized variants
         `:i32`/`:u64`/`:f32` etc.). A trailing `?` marks the column
         nullable: `(id :int, email :string?, ...)`."""]
    [body
      """**Row tuples:** each row is a comma-separated parenthesized list
         matching the column count. Values must satisfy the column type;
         a mismatch raises CXER0103 at parse time."""]
    [body
      """**Dictionary encoding:** string columns with low cardinality
         (default threshold: < 50% unique values) are automatically
         dictionary-encoded in the wire form, producing 5–10× smaller
         binary output. The threshold is configurable per-column via the
         `:dict` attribute on the column spec."""]
    [body
      """**Streaming:** tables larger than the configured row-group size
         (default 64 K rows) are emitted in chunks. Each chunk carries
         per-column statistics (min/max/null-count) in its manifest,
         enabling predicate-pushdown on read. See [[parquet-bridge]] for
         the Parquet round-trip and [[cxcol]] for the native streaming
         format."""]
    [body
      """**Arrow / Parquet bridges:** tables convert to Apache Arrow record
         batches (zero-copy via the C Data Interface) and to Parquet
         files. The bridges live on the analytics-bridge surface — see
         [[arrow-bridge]] and [[parquet-bridge]]."""]]

  [child id=schema n=2.8 title='Schema language (.cxs)'
    [intro
      """A `.cxs` file is a CX schema: an element-shape declaration that
         the validator can enforce. Schemas are CX documents themselves —
         no separate grammar to learn. Full spec in
         [`spec/schema.md`](../../spec/schema.md)."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[# users.cxs #]
         [schema
           [element name=user
             [attribute name=id    type=int    required=true]
             [attribute name=email type=string required=true]
             [attribute name=active type=bool  default=true]]]"""]

    [child id=schema-modes n=2.8.1 title='Open / strict / closed modes'
      [body
        """Schemas declare one of three validation modes:"""]
      [list
        [item """**open** — the schema's declarations are required; extra
                attributes / extra child elements not declared are
                permitted silently. Use this for forward-compatible
                evolution."""]
        [item """**strict** — declarations are required; extras are
                reported as warnings (CXLW001). Use for review pipelines
                where extras likely indicate a typo or a forgotten
                schema update."""]
        [item """**closed** — declarations are required; extras are
                errors (CXER0103). Use for security-critical surfaces
                where the schema is the authoritative contract."""]]
      [body
        """The default mode is open. Override per-schema with
           `[schema mode=strict ...]` or per-element. See
           [`spec/schema.md`](../../spec/schema.md) §9 for the full
           normative behavior."""]]

    [child id=schema-constraints n=2.8.2 title='Constraint vocabulary'
      [body
        """Beyond type-tag matching, schemas support a constraint
           vocabulary on attributes and element bodies:"""]
      [list
        [item """`pattern='regex'` — attribute value matches the regex"""]
        [item """`min=N` / `max=N` — numeric bounds"""]
        [item """`min-length=N` / `max-length=N` — string length"""]
        [item """`enum=(a, b, c)` — value is one of the listed atoms"""]
        [item """`one-of=(...)` — value matches one of N sub-schemas"""]
        [item """`required=true|false` — attribute presence"""]
        [item """`occurs='0..*'` — child element cardinality (XSD form)"""]]
      [body
        """Constraint violations produce CXER01xx errors with the failing
           constraint identified in the error context. Full vocabulary +
           error-code map: [`spec/schema.md`](../../spec/schema.md) §7
           + §12."""]]

    [child id=schema-defaults n=2.8.3 title='Defaults and inheritance'
      [body
        """An attribute with a `default=` slot is materialized at
           validation time if the input does not provide it. Defaults
           round-trip explicitly through canonical bytes — the
           materialized attribute IS part of the document after
           validation. To omit defaults from canonical form, validate
           with `--no-default-materialize` (lossless form only).
           Schemas can compose via `[?cx include]` (see [[includes]]);
           imported schemas' declarations layer over the importing
           schema's."""]]

    [child id=schema-binding n=2.8.4 title='Binding a schema to a document'
      [body
        """Three ways to associate a `.cxs` schema with a document:"""]
      [list
        [item """**In-document directive:** prepend
                `[?cx schema='users.cxs']` to the document. The validator
                resolves the path relative to the document."""]
        [item """**CLI flag:** `cx validate doc.cx --schema users.cxs`."""]
        [item """**Convention:** if `doc.cx` has a sibling `doc.cxs`,
                tools default to using it (override with `--no-implicit-schema`)."""]]
      [body
        """Schema-driven binary encoding (see [[data-bin]]) uses the
           schema's field order and types to omit tag bytes and field
           names from the wire form — producing wire sizes comparable to
           Protobuf or Avro."""]]]

  [child id=includes n=2.9 title='Include resolver'
    [intro
      """`[?cx include]` resolves a path to another CX document and
         splices its contents in place. Full resolver model in
         [`spec/include.md`](../../spec/include.md)."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[config
           [?cx include path='base.cx']
           [override port=9090]]"""]

    [child id=include-paths n=2.9.1 title='Path resolution'
      [body
        """Include paths are resolved relative to the **including
           document's directory**, not the working directory. Absolute
           paths are accepted but flagged by `cx lint` (recommendation:
           prefer relative). HTTPS URLs are accepted when the network
           capability bit is enabled — see [[include-capabilities]]."""]
      [body
        """The resolver honors a `CX_INCLUDE_PATH` environment variable
           (a colon-separated list of search roots), mirroring `gcc -I`.
           Paths inside the document's own subtree resolve first; the
           search path is consulted only if the local lookup fails."""]]

    [child id=include-cycles n=2.9.2 title='Cycle detection'
      [body
        """A include cycle (A includes B which includes A) raises CXER0140
           at the second visit to A. The resolver tracks include
           ancestors per document; sibling includes that happen to
           reference the same file are fine, only cyclic ancestry
           errors."""]]

    [child id=include-depth n=2.9.3 title='Depth limit'
      [body
        """Default maximum include nesting depth is 16. Exceeding it
           raises CXER0141. The limit prevents pathological documents
           from exhausting the parser's stack. Override with
           `CX_INCLUDE_MAX_DEPTH=N` (rarely needed in practice)."""]]

    [child id=include-capabilities n=2.9.4 title='Capability gating'
      [body
        """Includes are gated by capability bits per
           [[concepts]]: `include-local` (file-system reads under the
           document's directory tree), `include-absolute` (file-system
           reads outside that tree), `include-network` (HTTPS URLs),
           `external-entity` (XML external entity refs). The default
           profile enables `include-local` only. Untrusted-input
           processing should disable all four. See [[security]] in
           [[concepts]] for the threat model."""]]]

  [child id=namespaces n=2.10 title='Namespaces (xmlns:)'
    [intro
      """CX supports XML-namespace-style scoping via `xmlns:` attribute
         declarations. The normative spec is
         [`spec/namespaces.md`](../../spec/namespaces.md)."""]

    [child id=ns-prefix-decl n=2.10.1 title='Prefix declarations'
      [body
        """`xmlns:prefix=URI` declares a namespace binding scoped to the
           declaring element and all of its descendants until a
           redeclaration ends the scope. A prefix is then a `prefix:local`
           qualified name on any element or attribute in scope."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[doc xmlns:dc=http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/
           [dc:title 'CX Spec']
           [dc:author 'Ada Lovelace']]"""]]

    [child id=ns-default n=2.10.2 title='Default namespace'
      [body
        """`xmlns=URI` declares a default namespace. Applies to the
           declaring element and all unprefixed descendant **elements**
           until redeclared. Per XML Namespaces 1.0 §6.2 it does **not**
           apply to attribute names."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[doc xmlns=urn:doc
           [section 'Unprefixed elements live in urn:doc']]"""]]

    [child id=ns-reserved n=2.10.3 title='Reserved prefixes'
      [body
        """Three prefixes are reserved by CX and cannot be redeclared:"""]
      [table columns=('prefix' 'URI' 'notes')
        [row prefix='xml' uri='http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace' notes='XML built-in; always resolves; xml:lang accepted as alias for cx:lang']
        [row prefix='cx' uri='https://cx-home.org/ns/cx' notes='CX metadata; cannot be redeclared; carries cx:lang etc.']
        [row prefix='xmlns' uri='declaration syntax only' notes='Never resolves as a name prefix']]]

    [child id=ns-equality n=2.10.4 title='Equality and canonical form'
      [body
        """Two namespace bindings are equal iff their URIs are equal as
           byte sequences (no normalization beyond percent-decoding).
           Canonical form rewrites prefixes to a deterministic sequence
           (`ns0`, `ns1`, …) so two documents with the same data but
           different prefix choices hash to the same canonical bytes.
           Round-trip preserves the original prefix names via the
           lossless canonicalizer."""]]]

  [child id=locale n=2.11 title='Locale and language (cx:lang)'
    [intro
      """`cx:lang` declares the language of an element's text content
         using a BCP 47 language tag. Inherited-scope: the declaration
         applies to the element and all descendants until a redeclaration.
         Normative spec: [`spec/i18n.md`](../../spec/i18n.md)."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[doc cx:lang=en-US
         [title 'Welcome']
         [section cx:lang=fr-FR
           [title 'Bienvenue']]
         [section cx:lang=ja-JP
           [title 'ようこそ']]]"""]
    [body
      """The `xml:lang` attribute is accepted on input as an alias for
         `cx:lang` (per XML interop). On canonical emit, `cx:lang` is the
         normalized form. Every binding exposes a `.lang()` accessor on
         Nodes that resolves to the inherited language tag at that
         position."""]
    [body
      """Bidirectional text direction (LTR/RTL) is inferred from the
         language tag unless explicitly overridden with
         `cx:dir=rtl`/`cx:dir=ltr` (Unicode bidi algorithm)."""]]

  [child id=doctype n=2.12 title='Doctype declarations'
    [intro
      """A `[?cx doctype=…]` directive at the top of a document declares
         the document type. In v0.8.0 doctype is informational metadata;
         the validator MAY use it as a default schema-binding hint."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?cx doctype='users-v1' encoding=utf-8]
         [users
           [user id=1 email='ada@example.com']]"""]
    [body
      """The doctype name is a free-form identifier; convention is
         `<name>-v<major>`. A future v0.9.x extension promotes doctype to
         an *active* declaration (auto-load the schema by name, validate
         on parse) — for v0.8.0 it is reserved for that direction."""]]

  [child id=encoding n=2.13 title='Document encoding (UTF-8, BOM, alternates)'
    [intro
      """CX source is UTF-8 by default. The parser accepts a UTF-8 BOM at
         the start of file (and strips it) but does not emit a BOM in
         canonical form. Alternative encodings are accepted when the
         document opens with `[?cx encoding=…]`."""]
    [body
      """Supported encodings: utf-8 (default), utf-16, utf-16le,
         utf-16be, utf-32, iso-8859-1, windows-1252, shift_jis,
         euc-jp, gb18030, big5. Decoding errors raise CXER0001 (parse
         failure) with a byte offset; the parser does not silently
         replace invalid bytes."""]
    [body
      """Canonical-form output is always UTF-8 without BOM regardless of
         input encoding. Round-trip through a non-UTF-8 encoding requires
         re-encoding the output explicitly (`cx convert doc.cx --encoding shift_jis`)."""]
    [body
      """The CLI and bindings accept stdin/file/socket input streams; on
         stdin the encoding is inferred from BOM if present, otherwise
         assumed UTF-8. Non-UTF-8 stdin without a leading `[?cx
         encoding=…]` directive raises CXER0001 on the first invalid
         byte."""]]

  [child id=streaming-data n=2.14 title='Streaming parse and emission'
    [intro
      """For documents larger than working memory, CX provides a
         pull-based streaming parser and emitter (callback-driven, no
         full-AST materialization). Normative spec:
         [`spec/streaming.md`](../../spec/streaming.md)."""]
    [body
      """The streaming parser yields top-level children one at a time via
         a write callback; the application processes each child and
         discards it before the next is read. Resident memory is bounded
         to one chunk plus a small parser scratch buffer (~32 KiB)."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[# Stream a 100 GB log file, count error records — never
            materializes the full file in memory. #]
         [?for [in $rec [?cx-read-stream 'logs.cx']]
           [where [= [$name $rec] 'error']]
           [yield $rec/@code]]"""]
    [body
      """Measured throughput: 353 MB/s sustained on JSON-shape workloads
         (gate 15 in [`spec/v0_8_0_status.md`](../../spec/v0_8_0_status.md)).
         The streaming threshold is configurable via the
         `CX_STREAMING_THRESHOLD` environment variable (default 8 MiB —
         files larger than this auto-select the streaming path)."""]
    [body
      """For columnar tables specifically, the streaming format is CXCol
         (chunked column-oriented binary, see [[cxcol]]); the per-chunk
         manifest carries statistics enabling predicate pushdown."""]]

  [child id=limits n=2.15 title='Limits and quotas'
    [intro
      """CX has finite resource budgets to prevent adversarial inputs
         from exhausting host memory or stack. All limits are
         configurable via environment variables."""]
    [table columns=('limit' 'default' 'env var' 'error')
      [row limit='Max document depth (nested brackets)' default=512 var=CX_MAX_DEPTH error=CXER0142]
      [row limit='Max attributes per element' default=1024 var=CX_MAX_ATTRS error=CXER0143]
      [row limit='Max body byte length per element' default='256 MiB' var=CX_MAX_BODY error=CXER0144]
      [row limit='Max include depth' default=16 var=CX_INCLUDE_MAX_DEPTH error=CXER0141]
      [row limit='Max entity-reference expansion' default='10000 chars' var=CX_MAX_ENTITY_EXPAND error=CXER0145]
      [row limit='Max recursion depth in [?for]/[?def]' default=1024 var=CX_MAX_RECURSE error=CXER0146]
      [row limit='Max parse time per document' default='30 s' var=CX_MAX_PARSE_TIME error=CXER0147]
      [row limit='Max canonical-form size' default='1 GiB' var=CX_MAX_CANON error=CXER0148]]
    [body
      """For untrusted-input processing (parsing CX received over a
         network), tighten these via the `--profile untrusted` flag on
         the CLI or `cx.config(profile='untrusted')` in the bindings —
         which preset-clamps every limit to ~10% of the default. See
         [[concepts]] §security for the threat model."""]]

  [child id=datetime-detail n=2.16 title='Date / time semantics — timezones, precision, leap seconds'
    [intro
      """Dates and datetimes are first-class scalars (see [[dates]]).
         This section is the developer-facing detail."""]
    [body
      """**Calendar:** proleptic Gregorian only. Dates before 1582-10-15
         are accepted but represent calendar-extension (the calendar in
         use today extended backward), not historical Julian dates."""]
    [body
      """**Datetime form:** `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS[.fff[fff[fff]]][offset]`.
         Optional fractional seconds at millisecond / microsecond /
         nanosecond precision. Offset is `Z` (UTC) or `±HH:MM`. CX accepts
         both ISO 8601 and RFC 3339 forms on input; canonical output is
         RFC 3339 with offset `Z` for UTC or `±HH:MM` otherwise."""]
    [body
      """**Timezone preservation:** the canonical form preserves the
         offset as authored (`2026-05-22T10:00:00-04:00` stays that way;
         it is NOT normalized to UTC). Identity comparison `=` compares
         the absolute instant: `2026-05-22T10:00:00-04:00` equals
         `2026-05-22T14:00:00Z`. Sort order is by absolute instant."""]
    [body
      """**Leap seconds:** CX datetimes do not represent leap seconds
         (no `23:59:60`). Input containing `23:59:60` is normalized to
         the following `00:00:00`. Bindings that need leap-second
         awareness use an external library."""]
    [body
      """**Host language mapping:** Python `datetime` (aware, with
         tzinfo); Go `time.Time`; Rust `chrono::DateTime<FixedOffset>`;
         V `time.Time`. Each binding's Layer-1 surface preserves the
         offset; conversion to UTC is opt-in via `.to_utc()`."""]]

  [child id=reserved-tokens n=2.17 title='Reserved tokens and keyword list'
    [intro
      """A complete enumeration of identifiers that have special meaning
         and cannot be used as bare element names or attribute keys
         without quoting."""]

    [body
      """**Reserved literal tokens** (always have their literal meaning):"""]
    [list
      [item """`true`, `false` — boolean literals"""]
      [item """`null` — null scalar"""]
      [item """`nan`, `inf`, `-inf` — special float values"""]]

    [body
      """**Type tag keywords** (after a `:` sigil in value position):"""]
    [list
      [item """`int`, `i8`, `i16`, `i32`, `i64`"""]
      [item """`uint`, `u8`, `u16`, `u32`, `u64`"""]
      [item """`float`, `f32`, `f64`"""]
      [item """`bool`, `string`, `date`, `datetime`, `bytes`"""]
      [item """`table`, `seq`, `arr`, `map` — collection type tags"""]]

    [body
      """**Directive name keywords** (the first token after `[?`):"""]
    [body
      """All 40 directives in the v0.8.0 registry — see
         [[directives]] in the code section. Notable: `for`, `match`,
         `if`, `let`, `fn`, `def`, `try`, `modify`, `cx`, `pipe`."""]

    [body
      """**CXPath axis keywords** (before `::` inside path expressions):"""]
    [list
      [item """`child`, `descendant`, `descendant-or-self`"""]
      [item """`parent`, `ancestor`, `ancestor-or-self`"""]
      [item """`self`, `attribute`"""]
      [item """`following`, `preceding`, `following-sibling`, `preceding-sibling`"""]]

    [body
      """**XPath/CXPath builtin function names** — see [[builtins]] for
         the full catalog (~80 names). Notable: `count`, `name`,
         `local-name`, `position`, `last`, `string`, `number`,
         `boolean`, `not`, `exists`, `empty`, `concat`,
         `string-length`, `contains`."""]

    [body
      """**Reserved namespace prefixes:** `xml`, `cx`, `xmlns` (see
         [[ns-reserved]])."""]

    [body
      """If you need an element or attribute named after a reserved
         token, quote it: `['true' value=1]`, `[user 'true'=yes]`. The
         single-quoted form is the canonical escape; double-quoted also
         works on input."""]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/concepts/{data-model, sigils, types}.cx
     during the Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24).
     Concept-page framing on the data model, sigils, and typed
     values that complements the per-feature reference above. #]

  [child id=concepts-fold n=2.18 title='Concept framing — data model, sigils, types'
    [intro
      """The reference above describes each feature in isolation;
         the concept framing below shows how they fit together —
         the one tree shape behind six surfaces, the five sigils as
         structural reach, the auto-typing rules that pick a type
         from a lexical shape."""]

    [child id=cxdm-overview n=2.18.1 title='One tree, six surfaces'
      [body
        """CXDM is the shape every CX parse produces and every emit
           walks. Whether the source is CX, XML, JSON, YAML, TOML,
           or Markdown, the parser yields a tree in this single
           shape; whether the target is any of those six dialects,
           the emitter walks the same tree back out. Downstream
           code never branches on input format because the data
           model has already absorbed the difference."""]
      [body
        """A CXDM node is an Element with a name, an ordered list
           of attributes, an ordered list of body items, and a few
           optional structural slots — anchor, merge target,
           declared id, data type. Body items are Text nodes,
           Scalar nodes, child Elements, or the collection literals
           — Sequences, Arrays, Maps."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[pizza #margherita :large size=14 crust=thin
             [topping cheese]
             [topping basil]
             Stone-baked since 1987.]"""]
      [body
        """Reading this element from the outside in: name `pizza`,
           declared id `margherita`, data-type annotation `:large`,
           attributes `size=14` and `crust=thin`, then a body of
           three items — two child elements and a text node."""]
      [note
        """Whitespace inside a body is significant when it sits
           between text characters and collapses to a single space
           when it separates body items. The canonical form
           normalises this so byte-identical content carries the
           same hash regardless of source author choice."""]]

    [child id=sigils-catalog n=2.18.2 title='The five sigils + bool sugar'
      [body
        """CX uses five sigils. Each is a single character in a
           fixed lexical position that gives a value structural
           meaning beyond what bare text can carry."""]
      [body
        """- **`@`** — attribute prefix in body position; reference
             to a declared id or anchor.
           - **`&`** — anchor declaration; names an element so
             others can refer to or merge from it.
           - **`*`** — merge target; folds the named anchor's attrs
             and children into this element.
           - **`#`** — declared id; stable handle within the
             document's scope; target of references.
           - **`:`** — data type annotation; pins the value type,
             overriding auto-typing."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[order #o1234 customer=alice
             [line item=@margherita count=2]    # @-sigil reference to declared id
             [line item=@pepperoni  count=1]]
           [?for [in $line //order/line] [yield
             [row sku=$line/@item]]]            # @-sigil attribute access in CXPath"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[defaults &shared timeout=30 retries=3]
           [server *shared name=api]
           [server *shared name=worker]"""]
      [note
        """The canonical form renumbers anchors so two documents
           that bind the same data with different anchor names hash
           to the same identity."""]
      [body
        """Two additional sigils — `+` and `-` — are syntactic sugar
           for boolean attributes. `+name` parses as `name=true`;
           `-name` parses as `name=false`. Compact form for many
           booleans."""]
      [example lang=cx """[pizza +vegan -nuts -gluten +organic]"""]]

    [child id=types-fold n=2.18.3 title='Types — auto-typing, sized, decimal, bigint'
      [body
        """CX values fall into a small set of atomic types — `int`,
           `float`, `bool`, `string`, `null`, `date`, `datetime`,
           `bytes`, `decimal`, `bigint`, `atom` — and
           arrays of any of these. Most values are auto-typed from
           their lexical shape; explicit annotations cover the
           rest."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[demo
             count=42                      # int
             pi=3.14                       # float
             enabled=true                  # bool
             name=Alice                    # string
             missing=null                  # null
             date=2026-05-09               # date
             start=2026-05-09T10:00:00Z]   # datetime"""]
      [note
        """**Leading-zero strings.** Tokens with a leading zero
           stay strings unless prefixed `0x` (hex), `0o` (octal),
           or `0b` (binary). Postal codes, area codes, account
           numbers, BIC codes — anywhere the leading zero is data,
           not a numeric value."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[zip 02134]               # string "02134", not int 2134
           [mask 0xFF00FF]           # int 16711935 (hex)
           [perms 0o755]             # int 493 (octal)
           [bits 0b1101]             # int 13 (binary)"""]
      [body
        """**Sized integer and float.** A colon-type pins the
           runtime representation and (for sized variants) the
           legal range. Out-of-range values fail at parse time,
           not at runtime in some downstream consumer."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[port :u16 8080]              # rejects values over 65535
           [balance :i64 -1234567890]    # signed 64-bit
           [ratio :f32 3.14]             # 32-bit float
           [count :u8 200]               # rejects 256 and up"""]
      [body
        """**Decimal and bigint.** IEEE 754 floats lose precision
           for money and high-precision quantities; signed 64-bit
           overflows for some IDs. CX has `:decimal` and `:bigint`
           for both cases, with binding mappings to each language's
           native big-number type."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[total :decimal 19.99]
           [user-id :bigint 18446744073709551616]"""]
      [body
        """**Typed bodies and arrays.** A colon-type annotation
           pins not just attribute values but body content as
           well."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[count :int 42]
           [ratio :decimal 3.14159]
           [oven-temps :int[] 220 240 260 480]
           [tags :[] cheap fast tasty]"""]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/reference/{schemas,includes,streaming,tables}.cx
     during Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24). Compact
     quick-references that complement the per-feature reference
     above and link out to the normative spec docs. #]

  [child id=reference-quickref n=2.19 title='Reference quick-refs — schemas, includes, streaming, tables'
    [intro
      """At-a-glance references for the four big data-language
         subsystems that get their own normative spec docs.
         Schemas validate shape; includes compose documents;
         streaming bounds RSS; tables carry columnar data. The
         primary children (§2.7, §2.8, §2.9, §2.14) carry the
         per-feature depth; this section is the cookbook-style
         summary."""]

    [child id=schema-quickref n=2.19.1 title='Schemas — validate before processing'
      [body
        """A schema in CX is itself a CX document. It declares
           which elements may appear where, what attributes they
           carry, and what types those attributes hold. The `cx
           validate` subcommand walks the schema against an input
           document and reports diagnostics. Three modes
           determine what counts as a diagnostic: **open** admits
           undeclared content as warnings; **strict** rejects it
           as errors; **closed** treats undeclared content as a
           hard error. The schema may set its own default mode;
           the `--mode` flag overrides it."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[schema
             [element name='pizza'
               [attribute name='size' type='enum' values='small medium large']
               [attribute name='crust' type='string']]]"""]
      [example lang=shell
        """cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs
           cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs --mode=strict
           cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs --fail-on=warn"""]
      [note
        """**Type catalog.** `string`; `int` with sized variants
           (`i8`, `u16`, `i64`, …); `float`; `decimal`; `bigint`;
           `bool`; `date`; `datetime`; `bytes`; `enum` with
           explicit values; `regex` with a pattern. The validator
           coerces and bounds-checks each value against the
           declared type."""]]

    [child id=include-quickref n=2.19.2 title='Includes — parse-time vs eval-time'
      [body
        """CX has two include directives that share one resolver.
           The **parse-time** form `[?cx include=path]` expands
           files before the document reaches downstream tools —
           use for static composition (config files, schema
           fragments, page templates with known paths). The
           **eval-time** form `[?include path]` expands files
           while a CX program runs; the path is any CX expression
           — literal, bound variable, or computed via filters."""]
      [example lang=shell """cx --cx --include-root=./conf main.cx"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[users
             [?cx include=lib/admins.cx]
             [user name=carol]]

           [?include 'layouts/page.cx']
           [?include [?concat ['layouts/', meta/@layout, '.cx']]]"""]
      [note
        """**Security and cycle detection.** Both forms reject
           absolute paths (`E901`), paths that escape the root
           after lexical normalisation (`E902`), and URL-shaped
           paths (`E903`). Cycle detection (`E904`) and depth
           limits (`E905`, default 8) protect against runaway
           recursion. Full error catalog in
           [`spec/include.md`](../../spec/include.md)."""]]

    [child id=streaming-quickref n=2.19.3 title='Streaming — event shape and throughput'
      [body
        """Streaming parse and emit move bytes through the runtime
           without ever holding the whole tree in memory. A
           streaming parse fires four event kinds: `element_start`
           (name + attributes), `attribute` (after `element_start`,
           before any body), `body chunk` (text or scalar value),
           and `element_end`. The same event shape works on every
           input format — XML and JSON sources produce the same
           events. Use streaming when the input is large enough
           that resident memory matters."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cx
           with open('events.cx', 'rb') as f:
               for event in cx.stream(f):
                   if event.kind == 'element_start' and event.name == 'error':
                       print(event.attrs)

           with cx.stream_writer(open('out.cx', 'wb')) as w:
               for record in fetch_records():
                   w.write(record)"""]
      [note
        """**Backpressure.** The streaming sink uses a
           `flush_after_bytes` threshold to bound buffer
           residency. Default 64 KiB; lower for memory-constrained
           environments, higher to amortise callback overhead on
           small-emit workloads. **Throughput:** ~340 MB/s on the
           comparable bench corpus (M2 Pro, libcx `-prod`)."""]]

    [child id=tables-quickref n=2.19.4 title='Tables and binary forms'
      [body
        """A CX table is a structural form for columnar data.
           Columns declare a name and a type; rows are records.
           The Table API exposes count, schema introspection,
           dump, and load — symmetric inverses with declared
           types preserved across formats. The on-disk forms are
           **ast-bin** (binary AST, cap bit 36 advertises v8) and
           **data-bin** (CXCol v1 — columnar, used by
           Arrow/Parquet bridges)."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[:table
             [:col name='size'  type='string']
             [:col name='count' type='int']
             [:row [size 'large'] [count 12]]
             [:row [size 'small'] [count 8]]]"""]
      [example lang=shell
        """cx table info orders.cx      # column / row counts, types, byte size
           cx table dump orders.cx --to=cx
           cx table load orders.csv --to=cx
           cx table dump orders.cx --to=parquet > orders.parquet
           cx table load orders.parquet --to=cx"""]]]
]
